        "faq_1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How fast does a Fast-Boil kettle boil water?",
            "answer": "<p>Chefman Fast-Boil kettles use 1500 watts of power to bring water to a full rolling boil in as little as three minutes — faster than a stovetop or microwave. They shut off automatically as soon as the water is ready.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between a Fast-Boil kettle and a temperature-control kettle?",
            "answer": "<p>A Fast-Boil kettle heats water to a full boil (212°F) and stops, which is all you need for black tea, herbal tea, coffee, and instant drinks. A temperature-control kettle lets you dial in a lower, exact temperature, which matters for delicate green, white, and oolong teas that can turn bitter at a full boil. If you just want hot water fast, Fast-Boil is the simpler, more affordable choice.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What size Fast-Boil kettle should I buy?",
            "answer": "<p>Chefman Fast-Boil kettles come in three sizes: 1L for one or two cups, small spaces, and dorms; 1.2L as a compact everyday choice for a couple; and 1.8L for a full pot to serve the whole household. Choose the smallest size that covers how much you pour at once — smaller kettles take up less counter space and heat a single cup faster.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I make loose-leaf tea in a Fast-Boil kettle?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Every Fast-Boil size also comes in a tea-infuser version with a removable stainless-steel basket, so you can boil water and steep loose-leaf or bagged tea in one vessel. The standard Fast-Boil models are boil-only and pair nicely with a separate teapot or infuser.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I clean and descale a Fast-Boil kettle?",
            "answer": "<p>Hand-wash the glass body and lift-out lid, and never submerge the electrical base. If you have hard water, descale monthly: fill the kettle with a 50/50 mix of water and white vinegar, bring it to a boil, let it sit 15–20 minutes, then rinse well. Regular descaling keeps boil times fast and water tasting fresh.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_6": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are Chefman Fast-Boil kettles safe to use?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Every Chefman kettle has automatic shut-off that turns it off once the water boils and boil-dry protection that keeps it from powering on empty. They're cETL approved, sit on a 360° cordless base, have a cool-touch handle, and are backed by a 1-year Chefman assurance.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_7": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do Fast-Boil kettles keep water warm after boiling?",
            "answer": "<p>Fast-Boil kettles bring water to a full boil and then shut off automatically — they don't hold it at temperature afterward, which is ideal if you pour your cup right when it's ready. If you tend to sip slowly or come back for refills without wanting to re-boil, Chefman also makes Keep-Warm kettles that hold hot water for up to an hour. For most everyday tea and coffee drinkers, a Fast-Boil kettle is all you need.</p>"
          }
        }
